From f9b19fb4f4c954708bd7a6e4c6f90c877a10361f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Origin Engine <engine@origingame.ai>
Date: Sun, 25 Oct 2026 18:41:28 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [gles] Timestamp queries on WebGL2 through
 EXT_disjoint_timer_query_webgl2

WebGL2 offers GPU timing through EXT_disjoint_timer_query_webgl2, but the
GLES adapter set TIMESTAMP_QUERY only for GL_ARB_timer_query, so on the
web the feature was never offered. The extension's TIMESTAMP_EXT counter
has 0 bits in Chrome (ANGLE's Metal and D3D11 backends, and WebGL's own
clamp), so queryCounterEXT cannot be used; TIME_ELAPSED_EXT works
everywhere the extension exists. Each timestamp write now ends the
TIME_ELAPSED query begun by the previous write in the submission and
begins its own; resolving a query adds the intervals back to the first
write of its submission (CPU clock). Results that are not yet available,
overwritten, or taken across a GPU_DISJOINT_EXT report resolve to 0, the
value of an unwritten query. glow's web backend never reaches
get_query_parameter_u64_with_offset (which panics) because WebGL has no
QUERY_BUFFERS and no QUERY_64BIT.

Amp-Thread-ID: https://ampcode.com/threads/T-01a1743a-1fde-7456-b0bf-95294b1f65a1
Co-authored-by: FAN <xiaomao2026@gmail.com>
